  {
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "I think this will be tricky to fix - it seems like the gufunc broadcasting mechanism is limited to adding axes, and the kernel implementation assumes that the broadcasted array will be contiguous. I had a try in a branch to fix up the broadcasting, but my solution implemented broadcasting with zero strides, in the same way that NumPy does (see https://github.com/gmarkall/numba/commit/ae96876cb29242699bfb904c2614300a3b9922f8). This gets the shapes and strides of inputs and outputs right for this example, but the result is rubbish because the distribution of elements to threads was incorrect.\r\n\r\nIt seems like there would be quite a lot of work to rewrite things more generally to support this use case - however, pinging @sklam for a second opinion.\r\n\r\nNote: this works fine on the CPU because Numba only has to compile the gufunc there, and NumPy takes care of broadcasting.",
    "created_at": "2020-04-29T15:30:46Z",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/numba/numba/issues/5640#issuecomment-621287661",
    "id": 621287661,
